     I've loaded the LD decay analysis skill. Here's what you need to run:      
                                                                                
     ---                                                                        
                                                                                
     ## LD Decay Analysis Command                                               
                                                                                
     ```bash                                                                    
     conda activate piggpa                                                      
     export PATH="/workspace/app/bin:$PATH"                                     
                                                                                
     python                                                                     
     /workspace/pigbole/.piggpa/skills/piggpa-G/ld/scripts/ld_calculation.p     
     y \                                                                        
       -i <your_plink_prefix> \                                                 
       -o <output_directory> \                                                  
       --max-dist 500 \                                                         
       --decay-max-kb 500 \                                                     
       --maf 0.05 \                                                             
       --fit-method hill-weir                                                   
     ```                                                                        
                                                                                
     You must replace `<your_plink_prefix>` with the path to your PLINK         
     binary files (.bed/.bim/.fam, without extension), and                      
     `<output_directory>` with your desired output folder.                      
                                                                                
     ---                                                                        
                                                                                
     ## What "50kb Windows" Means in Context                                    
                                                                                
     The LD skill computes pairwise r² between all SNP pairs, grouped by        
     distance. The PopLDdecay engine computes at **1bp resolution**, then       
     the script re-bins to **1kb intervals** (weighted by number of SNP         
     pairs) to produce a smooth curve. This means LD is calculated for          
     every distance bin from 1kb up to the `--max-dist` you specify             
     (default 500kb).                                                           
                                                                                
     The "window" concept is inherent in the binning — every 1kb bin is         
     essentially a window where LD is averaged. For 50kb analysis, the          
     results naturally include r² values at 50kb, 100kb, 150kb, etc.            
                                                                                
     ---                                                                        
                                                                                
     ## What You'll Get                                                         
                                                                                
     | File | Description |                                                     
     |------|-------------|                                                     
     | `ld_decay_data.csv` | Binned distances (Dist_Kb), mean r², and SNP       
     pair counts per bin |                                                      
     | `ld_summary.txt` / `ld_summary-zh.txt` | r²=0.2 and r²=0.1 decay         
     distances, Hill-Weir C parameter, Ne estimate, GWAS window                 
     recommendations |                                                          
     | `ld_decay_curve.pdf` / `.png` | LD decay plot: X = distance (kb), Y      
     = r², with r²=0.2 threshold annotation |                                   
                                                                                
     ---                                                                        
                                                                                
     ## Key Parameters                                                          
                                                                                
     - **`--max-dist 500`**: Compute LD up to 500kb between SNP pairs           
     - **`--decay-max-kb 500`**: Plot X-axis up to 500kb (auto-enlarges if      
     r²=0.2 >300kb)                                                             
     - **`--maf 0.05`**: Filter out rare variants (MAF < 5%)                    
     - **`--fit-method hill-weir`**: Fit the Hill & Weir theoretical model      
     E(r²) = (10+Cd)/((2+Cd)(11+Cd)) for smooth decay estimation and Ne         
     inference                                                                  
     - **`--chrom`** (optional): Run on a single chromosome for faster          
     testing                                                                    
                                                                                
     ---                                                                        
                                                                                
     ## ⚠️ Time Warning                                                         
                                                                                
     For a typical pig dataset with ~50K SNPs, expect **10–30 minutes**.        
     For larger datasets (>500K SNPs), computation may take **several           
     hours**. I recommend testing with `--chrom` on a single chromosome         
     first (e.g., `--chrom 1`) to verify it works before running                
     genome-wide.                                                               
                                                                                
